1. Proof that L_{n+1} = F_{n+2} + F_n

Let [1 + sqrt(5)]/2 = phi and [1 - sqrt(5)]/2 = psi
phi^{n+1} + psi^{n+1} = [phi^{n+2}]/sqrt(5) - [psi^{n+2}]/sqrt(5) + [phi^n]/sqrt(5) - [psi^n]/sqrt(5)
sqrt(5)*phi^{n+1} + sqrt(5)*psi^{n+1} = phi^{n+2} - psi^{n+2) + phi^n - psi^n
sqrt(5)*([1 + sqrt(5)]/2)*phi^n + sqrt(5)*([1 - sqrt(5)]/2)*psi^n = [([1 + sqrt(5)]/2)^2]*phi^n - [([1 - sqrt(5)]/2)^2]*psi^n + phi^n - psi^n
sqrt(5)*([1 + sqrt(5)]/2)*phi^n + sqrt(5)*([1 - sqrt(5)]/2)*psi^n = ([3 + sqrt(5)]/2)*phi^n - ([3 - sqrt(5)]/2)*psi^n + phi^n - psi^n
([5 + sqrt(5)]/2)*phi^n + ([sqrt(5) - 5]/2)*psi^n = ([5 + sqrt(5)]/2)*phi^n - ([5 - sqrt(5)]/2)*psi^n
([5 + sqrt(5)]/2)*phi^n - ([5 - sqrt(5)]/2)*psi^n = ([5 + sqrt(5)]/2)*phi^n - ([5 - sqrt(5)]/2)*psi^n
Hence L_{n+1} = F_{n+2} + F_n


2. Derivation of the limit of  the ratio of successive terms of the recursive sequences

Consider a sequence given by the recurrence relation U_n = U_{n-1} + U_{n-2}.
Let lim(n to \inf) U_n/U_{n-1} = L.
This is equivalent to lim(n to \inf) U_{n+1}/U_{n} = L.
U_{n+1} = U_n + U_{n-1}
Therefore lim(n to \inf)[U_n + U_{n-1}/U_n = L.
lim(n to \inf) U_n/U_n = 1 and lim(n to \inf) U_{n-1}/U_n = lim(n to \inf) [U_n/U_{n-1}] = 1/L.
Therefore lim(n to \inf) U_{n+1}/U_n = 1 + 1/L = L.
L + 1 = L^2
L^2 - L - 1 = 0
L = [1 \plusminus sqrt(5)]/2 = \phi, \psi


3. Proof that the number of double-bs in a "word" is floor[(n-3)/5] for n >= 5

W_1 = a, W_2 = b
W_n is given by W_n-2 W_n-1
A double-b is added to W_n if W_n-2 ends with b and W_n-1 begins with b.

It can be shown that every word for n >= 2 ends with b.
Proof: W_2 = b ends with b.
Assume W_k (k >= 2) ends with b.
W_k+1 = W_k-1 W_k.
As W_k ends with b and W_k+1 ends with W_k, W_k+1 ends with b.
Therefore by induction every W_K (k >= 2) ends with b.

It can also be shown that every even-nmbered word begins with b.
Proof: W_2 = b begins with b.
Assume W_k (k even) begins with b.
W_k+2 = W_k W_k+1.
As W_k+2 begins with W_k and W_k begins with b, W_k+2 begins with b.
Therefore by induction every even-numbered word begins with b.

Similarly, every odd-nmbered word begins with a.
Proof: W_1 = a begins with a.
Assume W_k (k odd) begins with a.
W_k+2 = W_k W_k+1.
As W_k+2 begins with W_k and W_k begins with a, W_k+2 begins with a.
Therefore by induction every odd-numbered word begins with a.

Combining the above, a double-b is added if W_n-2 ends with b and W_n-1 begins with b.
That is, (n-2) >= 2 and (n-1) is even.
Therefore n >= 4, n is odd
Every odd n beginning with 5 adds a double-b
Therefore, the number of double-bs = floor[(n-3)/5] for n >= 5
